Content & grounding (extends your list)

Gold-standard exemplar workpapers — a small set of sanitized "this is what great looks like" examples per workpaper type. These do double duty: grounding material for the platform and a benchmark for judging its output.
Canonical templates, annotated by section: which sections are system-drafted, which are analyst judgment, which are data-fed. Your ownership matrix says who owns content; this says where content comes from.
Source data inventory — what systems/documents feed each workpaper section (loan system, spreads, appraisals, credit agreements) and how they're accessed. IT will need this and probably assumed you'd provide it.
Glossary and definitions — risk rating definitions, regulatory classification definitions (pass, special mention, substandard), house acronyms. Institution-specific vocabulary is where drafting tools stumble first.
For the policy corpus: not just the listing, but the operating model — versioning, effective dates, and who refreshes it. A stale corpus quietly produces confidently wrong drafts.
Quality & acceptance

Definition of done / acceptance criteria per workpaper type — your review standards say what good work is; this says when a draft is usable.
An evaluation rubric with an error severity taxonomy. A misstated borrower financial and a style deviation are both "errors" but need very different responses.
A golden test set for UAT — real, sanitized cases with known-correct outcomes. The end-user walkthrough you're writing will feed the UAT scripts, so build them together.
Governance (the part banks get examined on)

The AI/model-risk documentation your risk function will want from you as the use-case owner: use-case risk assessment, description of human-in-the-loop controls, what the reviewer must verify and attest to before a workpaper is final.
Records retention and audit trail requirements for workpapers produced this way.
If examiners will look at this, a mapping to the Interagency Guidance on Credit Risk Review Systems showing the platform doesn't weaken independence or coverage.
Adoption & operating model

Baseline metrics captured now — time per workpaper, rework/re-review rates — or you'll never be able to prove the platform helped.
A pilot group and a feedback/triage loop for bad outputs, so problems route somewhere instead of into hallway grumbling.
A shared-vs-tenant decision log: which assets are platform-wide (house style, maybe) versus CRR-owned (review standards, definitely). The other two tenants will force this question eventually; better to decide it deliberately.
Requirements traceability from your CRR needs to what the BRD actually covers, so gaps surface before UAT rather than during it.
An ownership-and-cadence assignment for every artifact on this list. Style guides and ownership matrices rot fast without a named owner and refresh cycle.
